We see that the difference between means is 3.2918431 times the std_error! At a distance of $1.96$ (either way) the probability of this data happening by chance already drops to $2.5 \%$ !! At this distance of 3.2918431, we would have negligible probability of this data occurring by chance!

:::


### All Tests Together

We can put all the test results together to get a few more insights
about the tests:


::: {#tbl-All-Tests-tt-1 .cell tbl-cap='All Tests for AMAS Anxiety Scores' tbl-subcap='["t.test","wilcox.test","Linear Model with Original Data","Linear Model with Ranked Data"]'}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
mosaic::t_test(AMAS ~ Gender,
  data = MathAnxiety
) %>%
  broom::tidy() %>%
  tt() %>%
  style_tt(i = 1, j = 5, color = "black", bold = TRUE, background = "gold")


wilcox.test(AMAS ~ Gender,
  data = MathAnxiety
) %>%
  broom::tidy() %>%
  tt() %>%
  style_tt(i = 1, j = 2, color = "black", bold = TRUE, background = "gold")

lm(AMAS ~ Gender,
  data = MathAnxiety
) %>%
  broom::tidy(
    conf.int = TRUE,
    conf.level = 0.95
  ) %>%
  tt() %>%
  style_tt(i = 1:2, j = 5, color = "black", bold = TRUE, background = "gold")

lm(rank(AMAS) ~ Gender,
  data = MathAnxiety
) %>%
  broom::tidy(
    conf.int = TRUE,
    conf.level = 0.95
  ) %>%
  tt() %>%
  style_tt(i = 1:2, j = 5, color = "black", bold = TRUE, background = "gold")
